    <h1>ref Keyword in C#</h1>

    <p>
        The <strong>ref</strong> keyword in C# is used to pass arguments by reference.
        It allows a method to modify the original value of a variable.
    </p>

    <p>
        Unlike normal parameters, changes made to a <strong>ref</strong> parameter
        inside the method directly affect the original variable.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Why Use ref?</h2>

    <p>
        Normally method parameters are passed by value, which means a copy of the
        variable is sent to the method.
    </p>

    <p>
        Using <strong>ref</strong>, the actual variable reference is passed, so any
        changes made inside the method are reflected outside the method as well.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Syntax</h2>

    <pre>
methodName(ref variableName);
    </pre>

    <p>Method Definition:</p>

    <pre>
void methodName(ref int number)
{
    number = 50;
}
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Basic Example</h2>

    <pre>
using System;

class Program
{
    static void ChangeValue(ref int num)
    {
        num = 100;
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        int value = 10;

        Console.WriteLine("Before Method Call: " + value);

        ChangeValue(ref value);

        Console.WriteLine("After Method Call: " + value);
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
Before Method Call: 10
After Method Call: 100
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Real-world Example - Updating Account Balance</h2>

    <p>
        The <strong>ref</strong> keyword can be used when we want to directly update
        an existing value such as a bank account balance.
    </p>

    <pre>
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Deposit(ref decimal balance, decimal amount)
    {
        balance += amount;
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        decimal accountBalance = 5000;

        Deposit(ref accountBalance, 2000);

        Console.WriteLine("Updated Balance: " + accountBalance);
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
Updated Balance: 7000
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Difference Between ref and out</h2>

    <table border="1" cellpadding="10">
        <tr>
            <th>ref</th>
            <th>out</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Variable must be initialized before passing</td>
            <td>Initialization is not required</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Used for modifying existing values</td>
            <td>Used for returning values</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Method may or may not assign value</td>
            <td>Method must assign value</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <hr>

    <h2>Important Points</h2>



    <ul>
        <li><strong>ref</strong> parameters must be initialized before method call.</li>
        <li>Changes made inside the method affect the original variable.</li>
        <li>Passed by reference.</li>
        <li><strong>ref</strong> keyword must be used in both method declaration and method call.</li>
        <li>Useful when updating existing values.</li>
        <li class="text-primary">
                In C++, when a variable is passed using <strong>&amp;</strong>, the original
                variable can be modified inside the function.
        </liclas>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Interview Definition</h2>

    <p>
        The <strong>ref</strong> keyword in C# is used to pass arguments by reference,
        allowing a method to modify the original value of a variable. The variable
        must be initialized before passing it to the method.
    </p>

